WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:18.875
<v Jamie>Paramount just asked state attorneys general for one point eight eight billion dollars to bail out a merger that's already dead—which is like asking your bank to co-sign a loan for a car you crashed last week.

00:00:18.875 --> 00:00:49.845
<v Alex>Markets closed with the S&P down zero point five two percent to seven thousand seven hundred forty-five, Nasdaq down zero point three two percent to twenty-six thousand six hundred forty-four. The VIX spiked six point six zero percent to fifteen point nineteen—so we're getting some jitters, but not panic territory. Tech took the real beating today: Meta down three point five four percent, Microsoft down three point zero four percent, Adobe down three point seven eight percent. Meanwhile oil jumped on geopolitical noise, and the thirty-year Treasury climbed to five point three one, the highest in nineteen years.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/17/treasury-yields-federal-reserve-fomc-minutes.html

00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:59.855
<v Jamie>So we've got this weird divergence where tech is getting hammered but energy is having a party because the world might be slightly on fire somewhere. That's the trade right now?

00:00:59.855 --> 00:01:19.563
<v Alex>That's the trade. Chevron and Exxon are both up—Chevron at two hundred two point seventy, Exxon at one sixty-one point forty-six—because when geopolitical risk spikes, crude gets bid up, and suddenly oil majors look cheap relative to their cash generation. It's not complicated: investors are rotating out of expensive mega-cap software into something that actually prints money when the world gets tense.

00:01:19.563 --> 00:01:41.774
<v Maya>So the real winners today are the companies that make stuff people need when they're nervous, and the real losers are the ones that convinced everyone they needed to pay forty times earnings for a chatbot that hallucinates. Accenture down three point nine one percent—which is wild because consulting is supposed to be recession-proof, but apparently it's not 'AI-proof,' which is a term I just invented and I'm keeping it.

00:01:41.774 --> 00:01:49.282
<v Jamie>Disney's down three point fourteen percent too. Are people finally tired of streaming, or is this just part of the broader tech unwind?

00:01:49.282 --> 00:02:05.861
<v Alex>It's the broader unwind. Disney got caught in the crossfire because it's still priced for growth, and when rates go up and growth stocks get re-rated, legacy media with high debt loads doesn't look great. Bitcoin's up though, which tells you everything about where money's actually flowing—not into stability, into things that move.

00:02:05.861 --> 00:02:28.698
<v Alex>So the mechanism here is Paramount's offering state attorneys general a one point eight eight billion dollar bond—essentially collateral—to compensate them for the delay costs if the merger doesn't close by June twenty twenty-seven. The AGs sued to block the deal in the first place, and now Paramount's saying, 'Okay, we'll put money on the table if we miss the deadline.' It's a hostage exchange where Paramount is both hostage and hostage-taker.

00:02:28.698 --> 00:02:38.395
<v Jamie>Wait—so Paramount's essentially saying to the people suing them, 'Here's a billion and a half in case we lose'? That's not a settlement, that's a bribe with a legal disclaimer.

00:02:38.395 --> 00:03:00.293
<v Maya>No, it's worse than that. The bond only triggers if the deal dies—so Paramount's betting that by June twenty twenty-seven, either the AGs give up or the courts rule in their favor. And if neither happens? Paramount's out one point eight eight billion dollars on top of whatever they've already spent on lawyers, deal advisors, and the merger itself. They're hemorrhaging money to keep a corpse on life support.

00:03:00.293 --> 00:03:21.252
<v Alex>Right. And here's what the AGs are actually doing: they're not settling—they're setting a price floor. By accepting the bond, they're saying, 'Okay, we'll keep litigating, and if we win, Paramount pays us one point eight eight billion dollars plus the cost of proving the deal is bad for consumers.' That's a pretty good incentive to keep fighting. It's regulatory theater with actual cash on stage.

00:03:21.252 --> 00:03:35.329
<v Jamie>So the bond is basically Paramount's way of saying, 'We know this deal is radioactive, and we're willing to pay for the privilege of keeping it on life support through trial.' What does that tell you about the actual value of the WBD acquisition?

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:57.853
<v Maya>That it's not about WBD anymore—it's about Paramount not wanting to admit they overpaid or that the streaming wars have already been decided. Warner Bros. Discovery's stock is down, Paramount's stock is down, and neither company can afford to write off the deal as a loss. So instead they're just going to bleed money for two and a half years hoping something changes. The bond is a symptom, not a solution.

00:03:57.853 --> 00:04:20.063
<v Alex>And it sets a precedent: if this works—if Paramount successfully buys time and court sympathy by putting a billion and a half on the table—then every major deal in regulatory limbo will have a playbook now. Post a bond, drag out the litigation, hope the political wind shifts. The AGs just got offered a very expensive education in how much consolidation in media is actually worth to the companies doing it.

00:04:20.063 --> 00:04:28.509
<v Alex>Prediction markets are now pricing Paramount's deal for Warner Bros. Discovery at one-in-four odds it fails completely. That's not speculation—that's serious money saying this doesn't close.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/17/pskys-wbd-bid-has-1-in-4-odds-of-falling-through-kalshi-traders-say.html

00:04:28.509 --> 00:04:40.084
<v Maya>One-in-four is wild because that's the same probability I'd give to my coffee maker working on a Monday morning. Except the coffee maker costs ninety-nine dollars and doesn't require two state attorneys general to sign off.

00:04:40.084 --> 00:04:45.402
<v Jamie>So the bond Paramount just posted isn't confidence—it's insurance against the thing they're publicly betting will happen.

00:04:45.402 --> 00:04:57.289
<v Maya>Andy Burnham, the mayor of Greater Manchester, got catfished by someone pretending to be Trump's incoming chief of staff. They exchanged messages—actual back-and-forth conversation—before Downing Street found out and had to quietly pretend none of this happened.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyvj5zdjj2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:04:57.289 --> 00:05:07.925
<v Jamie>Wait, how does that even happen? Like, you're a politician with access to government networks, and you're messaging someone you think works in the Trump White House without verifying who they actually are?

00:05:07.925 --> 00:05:23.254
<v Alex>The bar for a plausible Trump staffer impersonation is apparently just 'can type and has a Gmail account.' Burnham didn't run the email through a single verification—no call to the State Department, no check of the actual staff roster, nothing. He just saw a name and started talking.

00:05:23.254 --> 00:05:42.649
<v Maya>And the best part? Downing Street won't even comment on it. They're just hoping if they stay quiet long enough, people forget that one of their own mayors was convinced he was having a direct line to the Trump transition team. That's not a security breach—that's a masterclass in how to make someone look gullible in front of the entire internet.

00:05:42.649 --> 00:05:49.844
<v Jamie>On the tape tomorrow: we've got earnings from some names you've probably never heard of, but the macro picture is what matters.

00:05:49.844 --> 00:06:08.300
<v Alex>Adial Pharmaceuticals and Nuo Therapeutics both report on August seventeenth—that's the biotech lottery. But the real signal is going to come from the data: we're waiting on CPI next week, and if inflation prints hot again, that thirty-year Treasury yield we just talked about is heading higher. The Fed's done cutting, and the market's finally pricing that in.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/17/treasury-yields-federal-reserve-fomc-minutes.html

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:23.316
<v Maya>Meanwhile, AMC Robotics and Blue Dolphin Energy are both reporting the same day, which is hilarious because one's trying to convince you robots are the future and the other's just hoping oil stays above ninety. It's the entire economic anxiety of 2026 in a single earnings calendar.

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:28.947
<v Jamie>So tomorrow's really about whether the Fed's actually done, not whether some robotics company beat on revenue.

00:06:28.947 --> 00:06:34.578
<v Alex>Paramount's betting June twenty twenty-seven is far enough away that regulators forget they're angry. Spoiler: they won't.

00:06:34.578 --> 00:06:40.209
<v Maya>One point eight eight billion dollars to rent more time—that's just expensive procrastination with a legal team.

00:06:40.209 --> 00:06:44.588
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
